# Rules for coding agents using @criptonlabs/agent-apis-solana

Applies when writing Solana SOL or SPL token transfer code.

- Query the kit (or the API) before building or sending a transfer: `solana_transfer_readiness`, `solana_token`, `solana_network_health`.
- Use `solana_prepare_transfer` to get an unsigned transfer description; the kit never signs or sends it. The wallet sets the recent blockhash (`recentBlockhash` is `null`), signs and sends.
- Check readiness first and surface its blockers; do not send when the verdict is not ready (`transfer-not-ready`).
- Read `meta.stale` on every response and treat stale data as possibly out of date.
- Amounts are decimal strings in the asset's units; never use floats. SOL amounts have at most 9 decimals. Addresses and mints are base58 decoding to 32 bytes; `mint` is a mint address or `"SOL"`.
- Free routes live under `/v1/solana/*`; paid routes under `/v1/pro/solana/*` (mostly prepaid credits; per-call x402 where the API offers it). Credit purchases and token rotation use `/v1/pro/credits/*`; the price index is `GET /v1/pro/requirements`.
- Payment is opt-in: `CRIPTON_TIER=pro`, `CRIPTON_SOLANA_PAYER_KEY` and a mandatory `CRIPTON_MAX_PER_CALL`; `CRIPTON_SESSION_BUDGET`, `CRIPTON_SOLANA_PAY_ASSET`, `CRIPTON_SOLANA_NETWORK` and `CRIPTON_SOLANA_RPC_URL` are optional.
- Never hardcode prices or amounts to pay; read them from the API (`GET /v1/pro/requirements`, `kit.prices()`, `kit.priceOf(resource)`).
- Prepaid credits: `CRIPTON_CREDITS_TOKEN` (a `clc_...` token, needs `CRIPTON_TIER=pro`) is sent as `Authorization: Bearer` on paid-tier calls. `CRIPTON_SOLANA_SIWX=on` (needs `CRIPTON_SOLANA_PAYER_KEY`) spends the wallet's credits with a Sign-In-With-X (SIWX) signature when no token is configured. `solana_credits_status` reports whether a token is configured, whether SIWX is on and the last known balance, never the token.
- Never hardcode, log or commit a secret key or credit token; read it from the environment only.
- Handle errors by their `{ code, message }` shape.

## Purchase limits

- A top-up needs its own maximum: `maxAmount` in `kit.credits.topup({ bundle, maxAmount })`, `--max` in `agent-apis-solana topup <bundle> --max <amount>`.
- `maxPerCall` and `sessionBudget` never apply to purchases; they limit per-call payments only.
- `credits.topupBudget` (`CRIPTON_TOPUP_BUDGET`) caps the total of the process's purchases.
- A credit token is shown once: store it in `CRIPTON_CREDITS_TOKEN` right away. A Solana wallet recovers its credits with `rotate-token` (`kit.credits.rotateToken()`), which revokes the wallet's earlier tokens.
- `solana_credits_topup` exists only with tier `pro` (`CRIPTON_TIER=pro`), `CRIPTON_SOLANA_PAYER_KEY`, `CRIPTON_TOPUP_MAX` (per-purchase maximum) and `CRIPTON_TOPUP_BUDGET` (per-process budget); it keeps the bought token in process memory and never shows it to the model. With a credit token already configured, it keeps using that token and does not keep the bought one.
- A `solana_credits_topup` error `payment-settlement-failed` with `details.eventId` means the API could not confirm the purchase: it may still settle and its amount already counts toward the top-up budget, so do not buy it again. Once the API reconciles the payment, the credits belong to the wallet and `agent-apis-solana rotate-token` gives it a token for them.
- Its result reports `tokenKept`: true when the bought token became the process's token. When `tokenKept` is false (a token was already configured, or the bought token could not be kept), the purchase still happened: `rotate-token` gives the wallet a token for its credits and revokes the wallet's earlier tokens.
